# A ball from a canonical 2-limb center that a kernel wrote (an object, so its limbs are stored) and
# a radius from the program, guarded (rule R3): what the library calls on make_2's center in the
# point of a T and on the running sum of an accumulator; the constructors of a center the program
# gives, whose limbs it may have written, call ball_make_2_entry, this listing on the limbs guarded
# (the owner's decision of 2026-11-23). The empty set when the radius is negative, -infinity included (n
# true); otherwise the whole line when the center is not finite or the radius is +infinity or a
# NaN (f false: lt(g, const inf) is false for a NaN), as a NaN bound given to an interval is (the
# owner's decision of 2026-11-20); and otherwise the ball itself (milestone1.md, "Values that enter
# from the program"). The ordinary case is the first arm of each select (ok = not(n)), as the
# scalar printer's branch over a fix-up wants.
kernel:        ball_make_2
input:         c = c0 c1
input:         r
output:        cw = cw0 cw1
output:        rw
requires:      canonical c
ensures:       makes_ball cw rw from=c,r
min_precision: 1
source:        constructors (Milestone 1 listing; a NaN radius the whole line, 2026-11-20)
license:       algorithm: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeMake.v ball_make_2_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   every output is selected after explicit tests of the center and the radius (ok, f); makes_ball states what comes out

g   = guard(r)
n   = lt(g, 0)
ok  = not(n)
a   = abs(c0)
f1  = lt(a, const inf)
f2  = lt(g, const inf)
f   = and(f1, f2)
k0  = select(f, c0, 0)
k1  = select(f, c1, 0)
s   = select(f, g, const inf)
cw0 = select(ok, k0, const nan)
cw1 = select(ok, k1, const nan)
rw  = select(ok, s, const nan)
